<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Æthelred I of Wessex</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/871">871</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Æthelred I was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> from 865 to 871, the fourth son of Æthelwulf to reign. His reign saw the arrival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-heathen-army">Great Heathen Army</a>, and in 871, the year of battles, he and his younger brother Alfred fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> repeatedly, winning at Ashdown. He died after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> 871 and was succeeded by Alfred rather than by his own young sons.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aethelred-i-of-wessex.jpg" alt="Æthelred I of Wessex" width="320" height="293" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C3%86thelred_-_MS_Royal_14_B_VI.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Æthelred was the fourth of five sons of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelwulf-of-wessex">Æthelwulf of Wessex</a>, and his mother Osburh was of West Saxon royal descent. His grandfather Ecgberht had become king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> in 802. In 853 the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and a confraternity book of San Salvatore at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a> names both Æthelred and Alfred, indicating that both brothers went. Æthelred first witnessed his father's charters as king's son in 854, and in 862 and 863 he issued charters as King of the West Saxons, probably as deputy for his elder brother Æthelberht.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Æthelred succeeded Æthelberht in 865. His wife Wulfthryth is known from one charter of 868, where she is styled regina, a title rarely given to West Saxon kings' wives. They had two sons, Æthelhelm and Æthelwold. When Alfred asked for his share of their father's property, Æthelred said he had found it too hard to divide and would leave the whole to Alfred on his death. After Alfred succeeded, the supporters of Æthelred's infant sons complained, and Alfred had the will read to the witan to prove his right.</p><p>In 865 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-heathen-army">Great Heathen Army</a> arrived in East Anglia and went on to conquer Northumbria. When King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgred-of-mercia">Burgred of Mercia</a>, his brother-in-law, appealed for help, Æthelred and Alfred besieged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nottingham">Nottingham</a>, but Burgred bought them off. In December 870 the Vikings occupied Reading, and Æthelred and Alfred were defeated there in early January 871. About four days later they won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ashdown">Battle of Ashdown</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asser">Asser</a>, stressing Alfred's part, says Æthelred stayed in his tent to hear Mass. Defeats at Basing and Meretun followed, and at Meretun the West Saxons lost many important men, including Heahmund, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sherborne">Sherborne</a>. Æthelred also adopted the Mercian Lunettes design for his coins, creating a common coinage across southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> for the first time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Æthelred died shortly after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> 871, which fell on 15 April, and was buried at the minster of Wimborne in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a>. Because his sons were still young, Alfred succeeded him, bought off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> and won a decisive victory at Edington in 878. Æthelwold later disputed the throne with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-elder">Edward the Elder</a>, launching his rebellion partly from Wimborne. Æthelred's descendants played an important part in governing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in the late tenth and early eleventh centuries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Æthelred I of Wessex” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>King <b>Æthelred I of Wessex</b> and his brother Alfred led the West Saxons to victory over the Great Heathen Army of the Danes at Ashdown, on the Berkshire Downs.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-8">8 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
